# Normalize text in the repository; let git pick the working-tree ending.
# No eol on the catch-all: this file ships to every consumer's ROOT
# (sync-to-consumer.sh FILES), so a repo-wide pin here would legislate line
# endings for the consumer's entire codebase — .bat/.cmd checked out LF is
# the known cmd.exe breakage. Pins below stay scoped to what the harness
# ships.
* text=auto

# Shell scripts are the exception: bash executes them and shellcheck lints
# them, and both want LF. Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true as its
# installer default, so without this line a stock clone there rewrites every
# script to CRLF and `./joharness.sh ci` answers with SC1017 "Literal carriage
# return" on every line - 520 findings and a red gate before any work starts.
# Later patterns win, so this has to stay below the rule above.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Markdown too: handover-context.sh reads docs/handover/*.md from the working
# tree, and field() exits on any line 1 not exactly `---`. CRLF checkout empties
# every field - hook prints `[?, updated ?]`, gate above stays green.
*.md text eol=lf

# Everything else the sync manifest ships, pinned by path instead of a
# catch-all (see the top comment for why not repo-wide). `upgrade` compares
# working-tree bytes between the canonical clone and the consumer; any
# shipped file these patterns miss reads as changed on every stock Windows
# checkout (autocrlf=true renders it CRLF) — phantom updates on every run,
# CRLF written into the consumer, normalized away at commit, phantom again.
# The selftest walks the manifest and goes red on any shipped file left
# unpinned, so extending FILES/DIRS without extending this file cannot pass.
.agents/** text=auto eol=lf
.claude/commands/** text=auto eol=lf
.claude/skills/** text=auto eol=lf
.claude/settings.json text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
